# v0.25.13 — post-v0.25.12 work

**Baseline**: `v0.25.12` finalised at `a63b94a90` (2026-10-21). Sections below cover commits accrued on `main` after it, per the release-tag policy (`AGENTS.md`: the version's changelog is finalised in the tagged commit; later entries go to the next version's log).
**Date**: 2026-10-25.
**Tag**: `v0.25.13` (pending).

### MCP-GROUPS-1 — MCP CLI pivot allow-list derived from the generated group list

- `02f0da5e5` — `aitbc_dispute_*`/`aitbc_energy_*` read tools and the generic
  `run_aitbc_cli`/`read_aitbc_cli`/`list_aitbc_cli_group` pivots answered
  `unknown aitbc group` for ten real CLI groups (`agent-task`, `dispute`,
  `energy`, `eth`, `health`, `liquidity-stake`, `operations`, `service`,
  `stake`, `unstake`): `ALL_AITBC_GROUPS` was a hand-maintained literal
  frozen ten groups behind the generated tool modules (SM-06). The
  generator now emits `aitbc_mcp_cli_tools_generated_groups.py`
  (`GENERATED_AITBC_GROUPS`) in the same `--split-by-group` run — covered
  by the existing `mcp-cli-drift` gate — and the server derives the
  allow-list from it plus a curated-only set (`update`, covered by the
  hand-written `aitbc_update` tool). Destructive-leaf gating is unchanged:
  every mutating leaf in the newly allowed groups was already classified
  by `DESTRUCTIVE_AITBC_SUBCOMMANDS`/`DESTRUCTIVE_AITBC_PATHS`. New tests
  pin three-way consistency (emitted tool modules == group list ⊆
  allow-list == live CLI tree) so a future group cannot silently drift.

### MCP-ROUTING-1 — MCP host routing refuses ambiguity and names the resolved target

- `acebe2f0a`, `63a1d5588`, `2a94d750a` — `aitbc_mcp_server.py`
  `_host_for_role` no longer lets an explicit `host` silently override a
  `role`: a call carrying both is refused (`pass either role or host, not
  both`, surfaced as a clear `ToolError` over the transport) so a wallet
  or write call can never land on a different node than the role intends
  (SM-01). A role not present in the loaded `mcp-hosts.yaml` map is
  refused instead of falling through to be used as the ssh target itself
  (previously `ssh … bogus-role`); over the real MCP transport this hole
  only fires for a Literal-valid but unmapped role, since the tool schema
  already rejects unknown role strings. Explicit `host` overrides stay
  allowed for ad-hoc operator targets but must match a hostname/alias
  pattern — no leading `-`, no whitespace or `=` — so a
  `-oProxyCommand=…`-style value can never be parsed as an ssh option
  (verified over the transport: the value was consumed as an ssh option).
  Every dry-run and result envelope now carries `resolved_host` and
  `role_used` (`null` for ad-hoc hosts) so a reader sees where the call
  actually went. Verified live over stdio JSON-RPC: `aitbc_wallet_list`
  for `customer`/`shop`/`hub`/`follower`/`customer2` resolves to
  node0/node2/hub/node1/hub1 with distinct wallet names; role+host and the
  option-looking host are refused without any ssh.

### ESCROW-ROUTE-1

- fix(cli): relay buyer-signed market escrow creates through the coordinator (`POST /v1/escrow/relay`) before the settlement-node retry, so customer nodes blocked by the hub nginx `/rpc/escrow/` allowlist can create escrows once the relay endpoint ships; a 404 (older coordinator) or missing client credential falls through to the existing hub/loopback retry. Refs #442
### MCP-DRIFT-1
- `generate_mcp_cli_tools.py --check` now formats emitted output in memory (same `ruff format` pipeline as the write path) before comparing, and verifies the committed split-by-group layout (mode `all`) like `scripts/ci/check-mcp-cli-drift.sh`; previously it compared raw unformatted text against the formatted committed files and failed on every tree. New unit test `tests/unit/test_generate_mcp_cli_tools_check.py` pins the format-then-compare behaviour. (Refs #443)

### BRIDGE-MONITOR-1: bridge-monitor misses sealed deposits while idle and SUBMITTED rows never flip after seal. Poll-loop watchdog (BRIDGE-MONITOR-1): every cycle now logs a heartbeat ("poll cycle done in Xs"), the block scan is bounded by BRIDGE_CYCLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (default 300, cursor holds and ALERTs on breach), a cycle exceeding the limit logs ALERT, and a frozen ETH head (stale LB backend answering fine but never advancing — the shape of the 25 Oct miss) alerts after BRIDGE_HEAD_STALL_SECONDS (600). ETH_RPC_TIMEOUT (10) makes the provider request timeout tunable. Status lag: the retry path stored the *derived* envelope hash in ait_tx_hash and never adopted the hash the RPC actually returned, so the seal probe keyed on a hash that can never seal — deposits stayed SUBMITTED after the payout landed (P8/C2). Both submit paths now share _adopt_posted_hash, which stores the chain-reported hash; envelope_hash keeps the derived anchor. Refs #448

### SELL-59-FINDINGS-1

- `aitbc wallet bridge sell` `--hub-url` now normalizes to the bare origin
  (`scheme://host[:port]`): the hub nginx mounts `/v1/bridge/*` at the root,
  so a path-suffixed URL such as `https://hub/wallet` silently 404d. Shared
  by the wallet bridge sell/status/float/settlements leaves and `exchange
  sell_admin`; scheme-less input gets `https://`.
- `GET /v1/bridge/sell/status/<quote_id>` embeds the quote's lock row
  (`lock`, or `null`): the quote row freezes at `locked` once a lock is
  recorded, so the settled/released/refunded state was invisible without
  querying `/v1/bridge/sell/list` on the hub loopback. `aitbc wallet bridge
  sell status` reports the lock row's status with `quote_status`
  alongside. Refs #451
### ESCROW-ROUTE-1 relay
- feat(coordinator): `POST /v1/escrow/relay` relays a buyer-signed `/rpc/escrow/create` payload to the settlement node from inside the nginx allowlist — the market-escrow counterpart of `POST /v1/payments`, which requires an owned coordinator job and cannot serve locally-minted market job_ids. The JWT subject's bound wallet must equal `buyer` (admin exempt), non-admin amounts are capped at `ESCROW_RELAY_MAX_UNITS` compute units (default 360,000,000 = 10 AIT), replays of the same lock or (client_id, job_id) return the stored `contract_id` without a second chain call (new `escrow_relay` table, alembic `b1e2f3a4c5d6`), chain 4xx pass through at the same status while timeouts/5xx map to 502, and each relay writes one audit log line (client_id, job_id, contract_id — never signature content). Rate-limited 30/min per client credential on top of the existing per-client limits. Refs #442
### OPTION-DOCS-1

- docs(scenarios): option-level CLI coverage census lands as scripts/dev/gen_option_coverage.py (reads the staging SCENARIO-PLAY-1 census + cli-tree dump, emits the per-option exercise report). The census fix masking <placeholder> tokens before the shell-operator split revealed only 5 truly undocumented required options; docs gained a worked 'aitbc market host' example (scenario 11) and 'aitbc wallet bridge deposit/withdraw' entry-point lines (scenario 59). Required-but-undocumented options: 0. Refs #450

### fix(cli): paid-job escrow to resolves node_wallet, not proposer id (Refs #455)

- `market run`/`market hermes` no longer seed the escrow `to` from HUB_PROPOSER_ID; the explicit `--proposer` flag remains the only caller-side override and `_escrow_create` resolves the custody wallet from the node RPC /health (warned HUB_PROPOSER_ID fallback).
- `agent-task hire` settlement wallet order is now coordinator escrow-config → /health node_wallet → warned HUB_PROPOSER_ID.

### ESCROW-RELEASE-1 release relay + CLI fixes

- feat(coordinator): `POST /v1/escrow/release` relays an escrow release (`{job_id, amount, job_tx_hash}`) to the settlement node's `/rpc/escrow/{job_id}/release` from inside the nginx allowlist — the release counterpart of `/v1/escrow/relay`. The chain route is node-signed with no caller auth, so the relay reads the on-chain escrow row first and requires its `buyer` to equal the wallet bound to the JWT subject (admin exempt); the amount is bounded by the escrow's locked amount for non-admin callers. Replays — a stored `escrow_release_relay` row or an escrow already released on-chain — return the stored tx_hash without a second chain call; a refunded escrow returns 409 (new table, alembic `c2d3e4f5a6b7`). Chain 4xx pass through at the same status, timeouts/5xx map to 502, and each relay writes one audit line (client_id, job_id, contract_id, tx_hash — never payload detail). Rate-limited 30/min per client credential. Refund stays settlement-node only — no refund relay. Refs #456
- fix(cli): the paid-job `software_job` record leg retries once at nonce+1 (re-signed) on the occupied-slot 400 — the sealed-state nonce read races the still-pending escrow lock; any other 400 is not retried. `market escrow release|refund` and the job/host release legs now try the hub RPC after a local refusal and print the real status (a 403 names the API-key cause) instead of silently returning None; the refund path logs when the coordinator refund is unavailable before falling back to the chain rpc. Refs #456

### ESCROW-RELEASE-2 CLI release legs on the relay

- feat(cli): every escrow release call site now tries the coordinator JWT release relay `POST /v1/escrow/release` first (`market escrow release`, host `_release_market_payment`, and the ollama/whisper/ffmpeg/hermes paid-job legs) via a shared `_post_escrow_release` helper next to `_post_escrow_rpc`. The two bare-release sites that send no amount read the escrow's locked amount over the local→hub GET (GET is not nginx-allowlisted) to satisfy the relay's required `amount` field. Fallback to `_post_escrow_rpc` happens only on transport/auth classes — no stored client credential, 401 (rejected/expired JWT), 404 (relay not deployed or escrow absent), 5xx, timeout/connection failure — each named in the failure list; a relay business refusal (buyer mismatch 403, already refunded 409, amount-over-lock/invalid 400, any other 4xx) is terminal and never falls back, so a refusal cannot be laundered through the weaker-auth rpc path. Refund stays on the rpc — no refund relay exists by design. Refs #461

### ESCROW-RELEASE-2 review fixes

- fix(cli): release-relay edge cases from the #461 review — a non-positive or unparseable metered amount skips the relay (it would 400 terminally) and goes straight to the rpc as before; a transport failure (timeout/connection, no HTTP status) retries the relay once before falling back — the relay dedups on job_id so a repost cannot double-release; and a bare `{"detail":"Forbidden"}` 403 (middleware deny-by-default on a coordinator missing the route's security-matrix entry) is a skip/fallback like a 404, while 403s carrying a real refusal reason stay terminal. Refs #461

### ESCROW-RELEASE-3 release relay 425 retry

- fix(cli): a relay `425 "lock not yet sealed"` (chain gate `_ensure_lock_sealed`, `Retry-After: 5` — the ESCROW_LOCK is still in mempool) is no longer treated as a terminal business refusal: `_release_via_relay` now waits and reposts the same body — honouring `Retry-After` (default 5 s, 10 s per-wait cap, ~24 s total budget) — safe because the relay dedups on `job_id`. If the bound is exhausted the failure is reported as a named *retryable* condition ("not yet sealed — rerun `market escrow release`"), still without an rpc fallback (the rpc would 425 identically and a customer node cannot authenticate it). Business 4xx handling unchanged. Refs #469

### ESCROW-REFUND-1 refund relay (coordinator jobs only)

- feat(coordinator): `POST /v1/escrow/refund` relays `{job_id, reason?}` to the settlement node's `/rpc/escrow/{job_id}/refund` — the refund counterpart of `/v1/escrow/release`, buyer-bound the same way (escrow `buyer` must equal the JWT-bound wallet, admin exempt; ownership checked before any dedup/stored return). Phase-1 scope per the #471 ruling: the coordinator `job` row must exist and be terminal-unpaid (`FAILED`/`CANCELED`/`EXPIRED`, or a `QUEUED`/`RUNNING` job past its own `expires_at`); `COMPLETED` is never refundable and a missing row (market-minted escrow) is refused — those stay settlement-node refunds. A sealed on-chain `software_job`/`completed` record contradicting the job row fails closed (409), as does a failed lookup (502); a `job_payments` row already released → 409 or already refunded → stored result. No caller-chosen amount — the chain refunds locked minus released legs clamped to the sealed lock; `reason` is allowlisted (`buyer_requested`/`job_failed`/`timeout`/`expired`) and forwarded verbatim. Replays return the stored `refund_tx_hash` (new `escrow_refund_relay` table, alembic `d3e4f5a6b7c8`); concurrent inserts return the winner's row. Chain 4xx pass through at the same status (incl. the 425 unsealed-lock retryable), timeouts/5xx map to 502. Rate-limited 30/min + 10/hour per client credential; every refusal logs WARNING. Refs #471


### ESCROW-BREAKER-1 relay calls opt out of the circuit breaker

- fix(cli): the escrow relay helpers now build their `AITBCHTTPClient` with `enable_circuit_breaker=False`. The breaker is per-client-instance (nothing is shared across callers), but `_release_via_relay` reuses one client across its bounded 425/transport repost loop — every repost counted as a breaker failure, so after `circuit_breaker_threshold` (5) answers the client's `check()` rejected the next relay POST before it was sent, the status-less `CircuitBreakerOpenError` was classified as a transport failure, and the release fell back to the rpc path (which 503s/403s on a customer node) — the escrow stayed locked until a manual relay POST (#472 finding). The JWT-authed relay is the only release path a customer has, so its own bounded repost budget is the flood protection; `CircuitBreaker` gained an `enabled` flag (check/record_* become no-ops) and the same opt-out applies to the create and refund relays. Refs #479
